1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
Can you remake one of the most beloved games ever made
without losing what made it special?

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:11,500
Nintendo is bringing Ocarina of Time back on Switch 2 —
but this isn't being presented as just a resolution boost.

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:20,500
The official overview promises overhauled visuals,
fully voiced cutscenes with expanded dialogue,
and a symphonic orchestral soundtrack.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:29,500
There's also a new twist on the game's most iconic tool:
the Switch 2 microphone can listen while you hum or play
melodies for Link's ocarina.

5
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:36,000
And there's a new Hall of Fallen Heroes,
where you can study defeated monsters —
or challenge them again.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Those are real, announced features.
But the hardest part of a remake isn't adding more detail.
It's deciding what should stay untouched.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Ocarina of Time helped define 3D adventure games
for a generation. Better controls and richer environments
can make the journey easier to revisit.

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:02,500
But atmosphere, pacing, and discovery
are what people remember.

9
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:12,500
So here's the question: should a remake preserve the original
as closely as possible, or take bigger risks to feel new?

10
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:20,000
The remake launches on Nintendo Switch 2
on November 5, 2026.
Which side are you on?
